by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-18T07:37:25Z

Thanks for this, and for your patience waiting on a review. The transport itself maps the Cloudflare API accurately, so most of what I pushed is registration and conventions.

The one real bug: `getPayload()` read `$email->getTo()` instead of the inherited `getRecipients($email, $envelope)`, which 16 sibling transports use. That bypasses `framework.mailer.envelope.recipients` and `EnvelopeListener`, so an application redirecting all mail in dev would still have delivered to the real recipients. There is a test for it now, and it fails against the old code with `to@example.com` where `redirected@example.com` is expected.

The bridge was also unreachable and unpublishable as it stood. I added the four missing registrations: `splitsh.json`, `Transport::FACTORY_CLASSES`, `mailer_transports.php` and the `FrameworkExtension` factory map. Before that, `Transport::fromDsn('cloudflare+api://...')` threw `UnsupportedSchemeException` even with the package installed, and the package would never have been split out. The `UnsupportedSchemeException` entry pointed at the transport rather than the factory, so I fixed that and added the scheme to its test.

Other changes:

- renamed `CloudflareApiTransportFactory` to `CloudflareTransportFactory`. Every bridge uses `<Vendor>TransportFactory`, including single-transport ones like Azure and Postal.
- `composer.json` aligned with the siblings: `php >=8.4.1`, `symfony/mailer: ^8.2`, added `psr/event-dispatcher` and `psr/log` which the transport imports, dropped `symfony/polyfill-php85`, added `keywords` and `homepage`, 4 space indent.
- dropped the `array_first()` call, so the polyfill is not needed. It also only sent the first `Reply-To` silently; it still sends one, since the API takes a single value, but without the polyfill dependency.
- CHANGELOG moved from 8.1 to 8.2.
- removed the `.github/` directory. No component or bridge under `src/` ships one.
- added tests for the envelope recipients, multiple to/cc/bcc and reply-to, inline attachments with `content_id`, and the custom host and port endpoint.
- squashed to one commit and rebased on 8.2.

Two things left for you. Display names are dropped for `to`, `cc` and `bcc` even though the API accepts `{address, name}` objects, so worth deciding whether to send them. And the service is in public beta rather than generally available, which is a question for the team rather than for the code.
